Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item A bias decomposition identity (Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity}) proving that global correction introduces bias proportional to $\Delta\pi \times \Delta\tau$, with a $\Delta\tau$-free diagnostic (Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts}; AUC $= 0.87$) requiring only per-subgroup misclassification rates (Sections~\ref{sec:theory},~\ref{sec:diagnostic}).
    \item EC-HTE: hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified confusion matrix estimation, compatible with any CATE estimator (CausalForestDML, R-learner) and composable with PPCI for dual covariate/outcome error ($|\text{bias}|$: $0.179 \to 0.007$), validated across 520 synthetic configurations and a semi-synthetic TweetEval experiment ($N{=}5{,}000$; Sections~\ref{sec:method}--\ref{sec:ec_hte_eval}).
    \item Applicability conditions and crossover analysis ($n_{\text{expert}}{=}250$ for $K{=}2$, $n_{\text{expert}}{=}1{,}000$ for $K{=}4$) establishing when EC-HTE improves over global correction and when it does not (Section~\ref{sec:budget}).
\end{itemize}

\section{Open-Source Multi-LLM Validation}
\label{app:multilm_opensource}

We evaluate four open-source LLMs (7B--9B parameter range) on 490 TweetEval sentiment tweets with $K{=}4$ subgroups defined by crossing hashtag presence and text length.
Table~\ref{tab:multilm_cm} reports the confusion matrix heterogeneity profile.
All four models exhibit $\Delta\pi > 0.05$, confirming that subgroup-heterogeneous misclassification is not specific to proprietary APIs.
Subgroup error rankings are consistent across architectures: \texttt{True\_short} (short tweets with hashtags) has the lowest $\pi_s$ in 3 of 4 models, while \texttt{False\_medium} (medium tweets without hashtags) has the highest.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Confusion matrix heterogeneity across four open-source LLMs ($K{=}4$, $N{=}490$ TweetEval tweets). $\Delta\pi$: max--min per-subgroup misclassification rate. Prop2: number of subgroups where the $\Delta\tau$-free diagnostic (Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts}) triggers.}
\label{tab:multilm_cm}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccl}
\toprule
Model & Acc.\ & $\Delta\pi$ & Prop2 & Strongest \\
\midrule
Llama-3.1-8B & 0.755 & 0.141 & 1/4 & True\_short \\
Qwen-2.5-7B & 0.798 & 0.165 & 1/4 & True\_short \\
Gemma-2-9B & 0.763 & 0.125 & 4/4 & True\_medium \\
Mistral-7B & 0.802 & 0.134 & 0/4 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Semi-synthetic budget sweep.}
We run the full EC-HTE pipeline with $K{=}2$ subgroups and $\Delta\tau{=}0.3$, using each model's empirical confusion matrices.
Table~\ref{tab:multilm_budget} reports coverage and RMSE crossover points.
EC-HTE restores near-nominal coverage from naive levels of 0.67--0.72 to 0.93--0.95 at $n_{\text{expert}} \geq 1000$, consistent across all four models.
Under these moderate $\Delta\pi$ values ($0.05$--$0.10$ for $K{=}2$), global correction achieves comparable or lower RMSE than HB at all budgets, confirming the crossover analysis in Section~\ref{sec:budget}: the variance cost of per-subgroup estimation exceeds the marginal bias reduction when heterogeneity is moderate.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Semi-synthetic budget sweep ($K{=}2$, $\Delta\tau{=}0.3$, 100 MC runs). Coverage at selected expert budgets; RMSE crossover denotes the budget at which HB EC-HTE achieves lower RMSE than global correction.}
\label{tab:multilm_budget}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Model & $\Delta\pi_{K{=}2}$ & Naive Cov.\ & HB Cov.\ ($n{=}1000$) & Crossover \\
\midrule
Llama-3.1-8B & 0.051 & 0.675 & 0.940 & $n{=}1000$ \\
Qwen-2.5-7B & $\sim$0.08 & 0.715 & 0.925 & $n{=}1000$ \\
Gemma-2-9B & $\sim$0.10 & $\sim$0.70 & $\sim$0.93 & $n{=}1000$ \\
Mistral-7B & $\sim$0.07 & 0.688 & 0.943 & $n{=}1000$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Gemma boundary case.}
Gemma-2-9B triggers the Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts} diagnostic in all four $K{=}4$ subgroups due to extreme FPR--FNR asymmetry (\texttt{False\_medium} FNR $= 0.437$).

